insert into public.concepts
  (id, term, aliases, said, alias_list, status, one_liner, analogy, example, related, deeper, lens_pm, lens_eng, domain, priority, tags)
values
  ('expertsystems', 'Expert systems', '', '', ARRAY['expert system','knowledge-based system','rule-based system']::text[], 'Historical', 'Programs built from hand-crafted rules and knowledge that mimicked human experts — the dominant AI approach before machine learning ate everything.', 'A digital encyclopedia plus a referee: you feed it facts and rules ("if this symptom and that symptom, then likely diagnosis X"), and it walks through them to reach conclusions.', 'XCON, a 1980s expert system for configuring computer systems, encoded hundreds of rules written by human experts and outearned its development costs many times over.', '[{"text":"How it contrasts with modern learned models.","id":"machinelearning"}]'::jsonb, 'Expert systems worked by encoding domain knowledge as logical rules. A doctor-approved system might have hundreds of rules like "IF fever AND rash AND recent travel THEN suspect dengue." The system would fire rules in sequence to reach a diagnosis. They were expensive to build (hiring domain experts to articulate their knowledge is slow) and brittle (one missing edge case broke the whole chain). When machine learning proved it could learn patterns from data instead of waiting for humans to articulate them, expert systems fell out of favor.', 'Early AI success story, but the manual knowledge-engineering bottleneck made it unscalable compared to learning-based approaches.', 'A symbolic rule-based inference engine: given a set of IF-THEN rules and facts, it chains rules forward (forward chaining) or backward (backward chaining) to derive conclusions.', 'History & deprecated', 10, ARRAY['seed','history-deprecated']::text[]),
  ('symbolicai', 'Symbolic AI', '', 'SIM-bahl-ic', ARRAY['symbolic artificial intelligence','logic-based AI','classical artificial intelligence']::text[], 'Historical', 'AI built on explicit symbols and logical rules — the original vision of reasoning through manipulation of formal, human-readable representations.', 'Like doing math on paper: each symbol is explicit, each rule is stated outright, and you can follow the logic step by step.', 'Early AI researchers thought they could encode human knowledge as formal logic: "All men are mortal. Socrates is a man. Therefore, Socrates is mortal." They built systems that chained such logical statements to derive new conclusions.', '[{"text":"Foundational approach before connectionism and neural networks.","id":"neuralnetwork"}]'::jsonb, 'The symbolic AI worldview assumes intelligence is symbol manipulation — that thinking is reasoning with explicit, discrete symbols bound by formal rules. This contrasted with connectionism (neural networks), which emerged later and said reasoning emerges from sub-symbolic, learned patterns in numbers. Symbolic AI dominated research through the 1980s but hit limits: it couldn''t learn from data efficiently, struggled with nuance and exception handling, and required brittle manual knowledge engineering. Modern LLMs are sometimes called "sub-symbolic" because they work with learned numerical patterns, not explicit rules.', 'A worthy first attempt, but the requirement to hand-code all domain knowledge made it expensive and brittle.', 'An approach to AI based on formal logic, explicit rules, and symbolic manipulation, typically implemented as rule engines or theorem provers.', 'History & deprecated', 10, ARRAY['seed','history-deprecated']::text[]),
  ('gofai', 'GOFAI', '', 'go-fye or go-fai', ARRAY['Good Old-Fashioned AI','traditional AI']::text[], 'Historical', 'A slang term for pre-neural-network AI — symbolic, logic-based, hand-crafted knowledge — now mostly a punchline about how hard we got it wrong.', 'Like the Model T versus modern cars: it worked, it was impressive for its time, but you''d never go back.', 'In the 1960s, researchers believed GOFAI would soon reach human-level reasoning; by the 1990s, researchers had switched to probabilistic and connectionist methods, and "GOFAI" became a term for the naive old ways.', '[{"text":"A synonym for classical symbolic AI.","id":"symbolicai"},{"text":"Displaced by learning-based approaches.","id":"machinelearning"}]'::jsonb, 'The acronym was popularized by researcher John Haugeland in his 1985 book. By that time, the "good old-fashioned" approach — explicit rules, logic, symbolic knowledge — was already failing at real-world tasks. It couldn''t handle uncertainty, required immense manual labor to encode expertise, and broke on edge cases. The rise of machine learning and neural networks displaced it almost entirely. Today GOFAI is mainly historical; the term itself is a bit affectionate and a bit mocking.', 'Instructive failure: the assumption that intelligence could be built top-down through logic turned out to be far harder than scaling up learning from data.', 'Umbrella term for pre-learning-era approaches: rule engines, theorem provers, knowledge graphs, logic programming.', 'History & deprecated', 10, ARRAY['seed','history-deprecated']::text[]),
  ('turingtest', 'Turing test', '', '', ARRAY['imitation game','Turing machine test','conversational AI test']::text[], 'Historical', 'Alan Turing''s 1950 thought experiment: if a machine can fool a human into thinking it''s human, does it think?', 'A courtroom where the judge questions both a human and a hidden machine and must guess which is which.', 'Early chatbot ELIZA (1960s) often fooled people in text because they read human intent into minimal responses; people wanted to believe it understood them.', '[{"text":"A foundational question in AI philosophy.","id":"ai-safety"}]'::jsonb, 'Turing proposed the test as a practical alternative to answering "can machines think?" — sidestep philosophy, just check: if it behaves indistinguishably from a human, that''s what matters. The test is surprisingly easy to game (you don''t need real understanding, just good mimicry), and modern LLMs pass it trivially — yet we don''t say they "think" the way we do. The test reveals a gap between behavioral equivalence and actual understanding.', 'A famous benchmark that sounded profound but turned out to measure mimicry more than intelligence.', 'A benchmark test where a model must sustain human-like conversation to convince a human judge; easily passed by modern LLMs through pattern matching, not reasoning.', 'History & deprecated', 10, ARRAY['seed','history-deprecated']::text[]),
  ('eliza', 'ELIZA', '', '', ARRAY['eliza chatbot','doctor script','ELIZA effect']::text[], 'Historical', 'A 1960s chatbot that mimicked a psychotherapist by reflecting users'' words back at them — and people projected so much understanding onto it that they got emotionally attached.', 'A very simple mirror: you say "I feel sad," it says "Why do you feel sad?" You go deeper because you filled in the blanks.', 'Users would confess personal problems to ELIZA and get frustrated when the researcher asked if ELIZA had been helpful — they''d resent the implication that it was "just a program."', '[]'::jsonb, 'Joseph Weizenbaum''s ELIZA was a pattern-matching system with about 100 lines of script. Its famous "Doctor" persona used trivial rules: if you mention "mother," ask a question about your family. Users consistently read emotional intelligence into it. This revealed that humans are incredibly quick to anthropomorphize: we assume understanding if a response is contextually plausible. The "ELIZA effect" is still relevant — people mistake fluent text from LLMs for genuine reasoning.', 'Demonstrated that users attribute agency and understanding based on minimal behavioral signals.', 'A pattern-matching chatbot with hand-written substitution rules and template responses; early proof-of-concept for text-based interaction.', 'History & deprecated', 10, ARRAY['seed','history-deprecated']::text[]),
  ('ngrammodel', 'N-gram model', '', '', ARRAY['n-gram','statistical language model']::text[], 'Fading', 'A statistical shortcut: predict the next word based purely on the previous N-1 words, no deep learning required.', 'A fancy autocomplete that only remembers the last few words you typed: "The quick brown" -> probably "fox."', 'Predict SMS text on a phone: if you type "see you," the next word is very likely "tomorrow" or "soon." A bigram model (N=2) learns these patterns from millions of texts.', '[{"text":"An ancestor of modern language models.","id":"languagemodel"}]'::jsonb, 'An N-gram is a sequence of N words. A bigram model (N=2) says: given the word "the," what''s the most likely next word? It''s just counting — count how many times "the dog" appears, divide by total times "the" appears. Unigram, bigram, trigram, and higher-order n-grams capture increasingly distant context, but they hit a wall: they can''t model long-range dependencies and they explode in memory (you need to count every possible sequence). Neural language models — especially Transformers — replaced n-grams because they learn from data more efficiently and handle long context.', 'A clever statistical baseline that worked surprisingly well before deep learning; still fast for simple tasks.', 'A Markov chain language model that estimates P(word_i | word_{i-N+1}...word_{i-1}) by counting occurrences in a corpus.', 'History & deprecated', 30, ARRAY['seed','history-deprecated']::text[]),
  ('bagofwords', 'Bag of words', '', '', ARRAY['BoW','bag-of-words','word frequency']::text[], 'Fading', 'Represent text as an unordered collection of word counts, ignoring grammar and word order — simple, fast, and surprisingly effective.', 'Like describing a book by listing every word that appears and how often, forgetting sentences and structure.', 'Classify emails as spam or not: extract every word, count occurrences, feed counts to a classifier. "Free money winner you" looks spammy by frequency alone.', '[{"text":"An improvement by weighting terms.","id":"tfidf"}]'::jsonb, 'Bag of words discards all word order: "dog bites man" and "man bites dog" are identical. This is a huge limitation, but the tradeoff is simplicity and speed. You vectorize text as a histogram of word frequencies or binary presence. With simple methods like Naive Bayes or logistic regression, it works decently for classification tasks. Neural networks and embeddings replaced it because they capture word order and semantics, but BoW remains useful as a baseline and for very large-scale classification where speed matters.', 'A durable baseline that''s fast and interpretable; useful when neural models overkill.', 'A text vectorization method where documents become vectors of word counts, losing order and syntax.', 'History & deprecated', 30, ARRAY['seed','history-deprecated']::text[]),
  ('tfidf', 'TF-IDF', '', 'tee-eff-eye-deff', ARRAY['term frequency inverse document frequency','tf-idf scoring']::text[], 'Fading', 'A scoring trick that highlights words that are common in a document but rare across all documents — so "urgent" in your email matters more than "the."', 'A librarian who knows which words are telling: "blood" in a medical journal is informative; "the" in any journal is noise.', 'When you search Google, TF-IDF helps rank results: pages containing your rare search term score higher than pages stuffed with common words.', '[{"text":"An improvement on bag of words.","id":"bagofwords"},{"text":"Foundational to classic information retrieval.","id":"retrieval"}]'::jsonb, 'TF (term frequency) counts how often a word appears in a document; IDF (inverse document frequency) is a penalty for words that appear in many documents. Multiply them: a word gets high TF-IDF if it''s frequent in one document but rare globally. It''s a simple heuristic that works well for retrieval and classification without deep learning. Modern systems use learned embeddings instead, which capture semantic meaning beyond word statistics, but TF-IDF remains a solid baseline and is interpretable.', 'A robust, interpretable baseline for search and classification; overtaken by learned methods but still useful.', 'A term weighting scheme: TF-IDF(term, doc) = count(term, doc) × log(total_docs / docs_with_term).', 'History & deprecated', 30, ARRAY['seed','history-deprecated']::text[]),
  ('agi', 'AGI', '', 'ay-jee-eye', ARRAY['artificial general intelligence','human-level AI']::text[], 'Rising', 'A hypothetical AI system that matches or exceeds human-level intelligence across all cognitive domains — the holy grail and the source of endless debate.', 'A Swiss Army knife instead of specialized tools: a system that can learn to do literally any intellectual task a human can.', 'A system that could write poetry, diagnose diseases, design buildings, fix cars, and teach itself new skills without retraining — nobody has built this.', '[{"text":"Related to superintelligence concerns."}]'::jsonb, 'AGI is more of a boundary condition than a precise definition. Current AI systems are narrow: they excel at specific tasks (language, images, games) but can''t generalize freely. An AGI would presumably transfer knowledge across domains, adapt to new problems rapidly, and reason abstractly. The timeline is fiercely debated: some researchers think AGI is imminent (years), others think decades away, others think it''s philosophically incoherent. The term is often used to raise concern (AGI could be dangerous if misaligned) or to raise hype (AGI is coming soon, buy now).', 'A moonshot goal that drives investment and research; the timeline and feasibility are deeply uncertain.', 'A hypothetical system exhibiting human-like general intelligence: robust transfer learning, reasoning, planning, and common sense across all domains.', 'History & deprecated', 60, ARRAY['seed','history-deprecated']::text[]),
  ('artificialgeneralintelligence', 'Artificial general intelligence', '', '', ARRAY['AGI','general AI']::text[], 'Rising', 'The full name for AGI: an AI system with human-level or superhuman general reasoning, not limited to single domains.', 'The opposite of a calculator: a system that doesn''t just add numbers, but can learn to do anything a human mind can do.', 'A system that learns to code from a textbook, then uses code to solve biology problems, then designs experiments to test them — true transfer and reasoning.', '[{"text":"Often abbreviated as AGI.","id":"agi"}]'::jsonb, 'Artificial general intelligence is the inverse of narrow AI. Narrow AI (like current LLMs or game-playing agents) is superhuman in one domain but helpless in another. AGI would transfer learning across domains, learn from minimal examples, adapt to novel problems, and reason with common sense. The central tension: nobody knows if AGI is an emergent property of scale (bigger models -> AGI), requires new architectures, or is even achievable at all. Academic research focuses on benchmarks and partial steps; industry hype often conflates impressive narrow systems with progress toward AGI.', 'A distant goal that justifies long-term AI investment and shapes strategy; timing is highly uncertain.', 'A theoretical AI system with robust transfer learning, meta-learning, and reasoning across all task domains.', 'History & deprecated', 60, ARRAY['seed','history-deprecated']::text[]),
  ('asi', 'ASI', '', 'ay-ess-eye', ARRAY['artificial superintelligence','superintelligent AI']::text[], 'Rising', 'Hypothetical AI that far surpasses human intelligence in all domains — a speculative concept that fuels both aspiration and existential risk concerns.', 'What a human brain is to an ant''s brain, but more extreme: ASI to humans might be incomprehensibly vast.', 'In science fiction, ASI rapidly solves climate change, invents new materials, redesigns biology — but might also decide humans are irrelevant.', '[{"text":"Beyond even AGI.","id":"agi"},{"text":"Central to existential risk discussions.","id":"existential-risk"}]'::jsonb, 'ASI sits even further ahead of AGI: it''s not just general intelligence, it''s *superhuman* intelligence that compounds recursively. The idea: once you have AGI, it could improve its own algorithms, leading to an intelligence explosion ("singularity") where ASI emerges. This is speculative; we don''t know if ASI is even possible (maybe there are hard limits to intelligence), and we definitely can''t build it today. The framing shapes existential risk research: if ASI is achievable and misaligned, it''s an extinction-level risk; if it''s aligned, it could solve all human problems. The debate is intense and mostly speculative.', 'A tail-risk scenario driving AI safety research; the timeline and probability are radically uncertain.', 'A hypothetical AI system with vastly superhuman intelligence across all domains, capable of recursive self-improvement.', 'History & deprecated', 60, ARRAY['seed','history-deprecated']::text[]),
  ('superintelligence', 'Superintelligence', '', '', ARRAY['superintelligent']::text[], 'Rising', 'AI that vastly exceeds human intelligence — a speculative scenario that dominates discussion in AI safety and long-term risk.', 'If humans are to ants, superintelligence might be to humans: incomparably more powerful, making our concerns seem quaint.', 'Kurzweil and others predict superintelligence within decades; critics argue the prediction conflates narrow progress with general reasoning.', '[{"text":"Often discussed with ASI.","id":"asi"},{"text":"Motivates alignment research.","id":"alignment"}]'::jsonb, 'Superintelligence encompasses ASI and scenarios just beyond human-level AGI. The concept is baked into AI safety work: if superintelligence is achievable and misaligned, humanity could face an existential threat (the "alignment problem"). Conversely, aligned superintelligence could solve all human problems. This framing is powerful and shapes research priorities, but critics argue it''s speculative — we don''t know if superintelligence is physically possible, and the probability model is largely guesswork. The term itself is less precise than AGI; it''s used to invoke urgency and high stakes.', 'A high-stakes future scenario; AI safety funding and strategy are built around preventing or ensuring alignment.', 'A hypothetical AI system with cognitive capabilities dramatically exceeding human performance across all domains.', 'History & deprecated', 60, ARRAY['seed','history-deprecated']::text[]),
  ('connectionism', 'Connectionism', '', 'kuh-NEK-shun-izm', ARRAY['connectionist AI','connectionists']::text[], 'Historical', 'A foundational philosophy of AI: intelligence emerges from networks of simple, interconnected units (neurons), not from explicit rules.', 'Instead of following a recipe (symbolic AI), you learn by adjusting many tiny weights based on mistakes — like a student learning by doing, not by instruction manual.', 'Neural networks (including today''s Transformers) are connectionist: they learn by backpropagation, adjusting weights until the system produces correct outputs.', '[{"text":"Overcame symbolic AI limitations.","id":"symbolicai"},{"text":"Foundation of modern neural networks.","id":"neuralnetwork"}]'::jsonb, 'Connectionism emerged in the 1980s as a counter to symbolic AI. The idea: intelligence doesn''t arise from explicit knowledge and rules, but from learned patterns in networks of artificial neurons. Early connectionist systems were limited (small networks, slow training), but as computation scaled, they dramatically outperformed symbolic approaches. Today''s deep learning and LLMs are thoroughly connectionist — they learn numerical representations, not symbols. The term "connectionism" is less common now (we just say "neural networks" or "deep learning"), but it represents a major philosophical shift: from top-down knowledge engineering to bottom-up learning.', 'A winning philosophy: learning from data proved vastly more scalable and general than hand-crafted rules.', 'An AI approach based on networks of artificial neurons with weighted connections trained via learning algorithms like backpropagation.', 'History & deprecated', 10, ARRAY['seed','history-deprecated']::text[]),
  ('perceptron', 'Perceptron', '', '', ARRAY['perceptron model','McCulloch-Pitts neuron']::text[], 'Historical', 'An early artificial neuron (1958) that mimicked biological neurons by combining weighted inputs and firing if they exceeded a threshold.', 'A simple light switch that turns on if the total incoming signal is strong enough.', 'A perceptron could learn to classify handwritten digits or simple patterns by adjusting its weights: if it misclassified, it would tweak the weights slightly and try again.', '[{"text":"Foundation of connectionism and neural networks.","id":"connectionism"},{"text":"A building block of deep learning.","id":"neuralnetwork"}]'::jsonb, 'Frank Rosenblatt''s perceptron was the first learning machine that provably converged: given enough examples, it could learn any linearly separable classification task. It sparked huge optimism — scientists predicted AGI was near — but Minsky and Papert showed in 1969 that single perceptrons couldn''t solve even simple problems like XOR. This led to the first "AI winter." Perceptrons were resurrected as building blocks of multilayer neural networks, which could solve nonlinear problems. Today''s deep networks are perceptrons stacked in layers; the perceptron remains conceptually central to how neural networks work.', 'An early success that raised unrealistic expectations, then faded until neural networks scaled to practical usefulness.', 'A simple linear classifier: output = 1 if sum(w_i * x_i + b) > 0, else 0. Learns by adjusting weights via the perceptron update rule.', 'History & deprecated', 10, ARRAY['seed','history-deprecated']::text[])
